00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:51.850
OK.

00:09:51.850 --> 00:09:53.890
OK, right.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:56.620
Data privacy is certainly
something to be careful of,

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.740
but we are very happy to
say that with Google Docs

00:09:59.740 --> 00:10:02.290
and with G Suite and
more broadly, you always

00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:05.320
have full control over who
has access to a document,

00:10:05.320 --> 00:10:07.360
and what they can do
with that document.

00:10:07.360 --> 00:10:10.540
So let's take a look at
some of these features.

00:10:10.540 --> 00:10:13.060
As you're probably aware,
with Google Docs, when you're

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:16.700
working internally, you can
share a document with somebody

00:10:16.700 --> 00:10:20.680
by giving them only view-only
or comment-only rights.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.560
And it turns out that you can
do this when sharing externally

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:24.680
as well.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:27.527
So if you aren't seeking
somebody's comments or edits

00:10:27.527 --> 00:10:29.110
to a particular
document, you can just

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.887
give them view-only rights.

00:10:31.887 --> 00:10:33.970
Let's say, for example,
that you did give somebody

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.706
external edit rights
to a document, well,

00:10:36.706 --> 00:10:38.080
it turns out later
you can always

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:40.960
come back to the document and
look at its revision history

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:45.430
to see exactly who changed what.

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:46.960
There are also
some advance things

00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:49.480
you can do when sharing
a document with anybody

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.970
internal or external, and
these kinds of settings

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:54.040
include things like
preventing them

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:56.080
from adding further
people to the document,

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:58.090
preventing them from
downloading the document,

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:02.450
or even preventing them from
copying from the document.

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:04.330
Another useful
feature for sharing

00:11:04.330 --> 00:11:08.120
is what we call
expiring permissions.

00:11:08.120 --> 00:11:11.050
So this is found in the Advanced
Settings of the Share dialog,

00:11:11.050 --> 00:11:13.690
as you can see from
the quick video here.

00:11:13.690 --> 00:11:15.190
And what this
allows you to do is

00:11:15.190 --> 00:11:17.530
to give somebody permission
to a document for only

00:11:17.530 --> 00:11:19.102
a certain amount of time.

00:11:19.102 --> 00:11:21.310
So if you don't want to give
them permission forever,

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:25.120
this is the easy way to do it

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:27.520
And the final sharing
feature we wanted to bring up

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:31.900
is Drive's data loss
prevention feature called DLP.

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.390
So with this, a domain
admin can actually

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:38.230
set predefined content
detectors over the entire corpus

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:40.180
of your company's documents.

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.100
When one of these
detectors detects

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:44.050
what it was set
up to find, it'll

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.000
actually enforce certain
sharing rules on that document.

00:11:47.000 --> 00:11:50.860
So for example, you might set
a rule that says if a document

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:54.070
has the word confidential in it
or if a document has what looks

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.160
like a credit card number in it,
you can actually say that those

00:11:57.160 --> 00:12:00.950
documents cannot be
shared externally, period.

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:04.180
So this is a handy tool
for admins to up the data

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.017
security a little bit.

00:12:06.017 --> 00:12:07.600
So with all of these
sharing features,

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:10.420
we always strive to
give you full control

00:12:10.420 --> 00:12:12.670
over exactly who has
access to your documents,

00:12:12.670 --> 00:12:15.320
and what they can do with it.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:22.590
So we have successfully shared
our document externally, no?

00:12:22.590 --> 00:12:26.667
And our collaborators at RGB
are taking a pass over it.

00:12:26.667 --> 00:12:28.500
It seems like the video's
not quite working.

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.050
But let's imagine that
our collaborators at RGB

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:33.210
are taking a pass
over this document.

00:12:33.210 --> 00:12:35.760
So in the past, what
would have happened

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:38.490
is that they would've
added comments,

00:12:38.490 --> 00:12:41.730
they would've made some
revisions to the document,

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:44.760
and then they would have sent
over a new version to you.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:47.040
Sort of, like a giant v2
with a lot of things for you

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.095
to process.

00:12:48.095 --> 00:12:50.579
But it turns out that
with Google Docs,

00:12:50.579 --> 00:12:52.620
all the same kinds of
collaboration features used

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.800
internally are available
for use externally as well.

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:59.940
So imagine, for
example, that some

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:01.800
of our partners
from RGB suppliers

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:03.390
are working through
this document.

00:13:03.390 --> 00:13:06.210
They are going through, they're
leaving comments, which Vishnu

00:13:06.210 --> 00:13:08.430
pointed out earlier,
but they can also

00:13:08.430 --> 00:13:11.850
make suggested changes, which
should remind you of, sort of,

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:15.345
the red lining capabilities
that you might be familiar with.

00:13:15.345 --> 00:13:17.940
With all of these
suggested changes,

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.190
any changes from
one given author

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:22.740
will show up to you
in a particular color,

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:25.260
and the exact change
that they're proposing

00:13:25.260 --> 00:13:27.120
will show up to you
on the right-hand side

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.040
near any comments.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.080
For each individual
suggestion, you

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:34.800
can choose to accept or
reject it in real time,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:36.930
or you can come back
later to the document

00:13:36.930 --> 00:13:39.990
and do a full pass
over all suggestions.

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:42.840
Coming soon is a
new feature, which

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.640
will allow you to
accept or reject

00:13:44.640 --> 00:13:46.749
all the changes on
a document at once,

00:13:46.749 --> 00:13:48.540
and you'll also have
the ability to preview

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.820
the document as if all
changes were either

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:52.540
accepted or rejected.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:54.790
So these are improvements
on the traditional redlining

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.240
experience that we think will
help you become even more

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:01.460
efficient when dealing
with documents like this.

00:14:01.460 --> 00:14:04.680
We have some happy
lawyers in the audience.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:05.180
Great.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.100
So, so far we have
been talking just

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:09.560
about collaborating
between G Suite

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.750
users, which was a
great experience that we

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.160
care very much about.

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:16.040
But we know realistically
that a lot of times

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:18.200
your legal teams might be
collaborating with people

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:21.205
who are still using
Word, and we are

00:14:21.205 --> 00:14:22.580
very happy to tell
you here today

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:23.880
that that's completely fine.

00:14:23.880 --> 00:14:26.180
We don't need to
hold it against them.

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:29.990
So on the left here, you see
a document by our friends

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:31.610
over at RGB Suppliers.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.389
They're still using
Word, and you'll

00:14:33.389 --> 00:14:35.930
notice that this document looks
like a legal contract, right.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:38.609
It has some particular
formatting about it.

00:14:38.609 --> 00:14:40.400
It looks like it came
from an RGB template,

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:42.680
because it has a blue
header across the top,

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.720
and there are even
a couple of comments

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.670
in here already for us.

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:50.090
So our counterparts
at RGB save this file

00:14:50.090 --> 00:14:55.160
and email it over to us, and we
open this file in Google Docs.

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:58.560
And we get what we
see on the right here.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.210
So you notice a few things.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:04.230
First is that the formatting
and content are preserved.

00:15:04.230 --> 00:15:07.080
You even get the little
blue bar across the top.

00:15:07.080 --> 00:15:09.350
And you'll notice that the
comments originally in word

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:12.290
show up as native
Google Docs comments,

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.410
so you can do the threaded
conversations and the assigned

00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:18.800
action items that Vishnu
was talking about earlier.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.360
And why do we invest in this?

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:23.390
We invest in this, because we
know that a lot of our users

00:15:23.390 --> 00:15:26.120
are still interacting with
people who are on Office,

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.880
and so we want to make
file conversion fidelity

00:15:28.880 --> 00:15:32.150
something that's a very high
quality feature within Google

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:34.850
Docs.

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:35.350
Great.

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:38.380
So we've settled on the
contents and the formatting

00:15:38.380 --> 00:15:39.070
of a document.

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:41.440
And now we want
to get signatures

00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:44.770
from both parties on this
particular legal contract.

00:15:44.770 --> 00:15:47.260
It turns out that
the G Suite ecosystem

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:50.740
has a lot of partners
who are building out

00:15:50.740 --> 00:15:52.940
high-quality integrations
between parts of G

00:15:52.940 --> 00:15:54.850
Suite and their own products.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:56.560
And this is great
for us, because

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:58.310
for our electronic
signature needs,

00:15:58.310 --> 00:16:01.720
for example, we might
choose to use Docusign.

00:16:01.720 --> 00:16:03.520
Docusign is a
solution that works.

00:16:03.520 --> 00:16:08.560
It has over 100 million
users in 188 countries.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:12.040
And moreover, it's a
recommended for G Suite partner.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:15.820
So all recommended partners have
to go through certain security

00:16:15.820 --> 00:16:18.460
and integration
standards reviews.

00:16:18.460 --> 00:16:21.250
And this is because we know that
when dealing with a third party

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:25.940
ecosystem, data security
becomes even more important.

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:27.760
So Docusign has a
number of integrations

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:29.869
with different parts
of the suite already,

00:16:29.869 --> 00:16:31.660
but we are excited to
announce that they're

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:33.210
building even more.

00:16:33.210 --> 00:16:35.110
So towards the end
of last year, they

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:37.790
released their new
Android add-ons,

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:40.210
so you can sign from
your Android device.

00:16:40.210 --> 00:16:43.360
And here on Next, we are
very excited to preannounce

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.720
their new Docs add-on, which
is what you see in the sidebar

00:16:46.720 --> 00:16:47.770
here.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:49.930
With this new Docs
add-on, users will

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:53.260
be able to finish editing
a file in Google Docs,

00:16:53.260 --> 00:16:56.050
and then they will be able to
begin the Docusign signature

00:16:56.050 --> 00:16:58.600
experience via this add-on.

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:00.400
This is what we mean
by making workflows

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:03.180
more efficient for our users.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:06.030
Moreover, this new
Docs add-on will

00:17:06.030 --> 00:17:08.509
be making an appearance
in another new feature

00:17:08.509 --> 00:17:10.050
that we're preannouncing
here on Next

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.480
called Template Integrations.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:15.599
Template Integration is a
pairing of a specific template

00:17:15.599 --> 00:17:17.490
with a specific add-on.

00:17:17.490 --> 00:17:21.150
So why might this be useful
for legal use cases, you say?

00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:23.339
So let's say, for
example, your company has

00:17:23.339 --> 00:17:26.369
a number of standardized
legal contracts

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:29.640
that you want all your
employees to have access to.

00:17:29.640 --> 00:17:31.710
Moreover, let's assume
that your company

00:17:31.710 --> 00:17:35.760
uses Docusign for its
electronic signature needs.

00:17:35.760 --> 00:17:38.220
If you were to take
those legal contracts

00:17:38.220 --> 00:17:40.060
and upload them to
your template gallery

00:17:40.060 --> 00:17:43.410
that Vishnu described earlier,
and if you paired them

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:47.512
with the Docusign add-on, all of
a sudden all of your employees

00:17:47.512 --> 00:17:49.470
who are creating documents
from those templates

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:52.080
will know exactly the right
electronic signature tool

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.960
to use, and they will have
it at their fingertips

00:17:54.960 --> 00:17:56.860
when they're ready.

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:59.282
So we're very excited about
this partnership with Docusign

00:17:59.282 --> 00:18:01.740
and with other partners that
could help with your legal use

00:18:01.740 --> 00:18:03.840
cases.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:06.480
We also wanted to point out
that it is possible for you

00:18:06.480 --> 00:18:10.920
to create your own extensions
to G Suite via App Script.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.710
If you want some ideas
on different tools

00:18:13.710 --> 00:18:15.270
to build out using
App Script, feel

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:18.000
free to visit our public web
page, known as the G Suite

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:20.400
Transformation Gallery.

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:22.020
But for example,
you might consider

00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:24.810
building a Docs add-on
that lets you paste

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:28.590
standardized clauses of
legal contracts into Docs,

00:18:28.590 --> 00:18:30.900
or you could build a tool
with Apps Script that

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:33.810
helps you keep track of
exactly who has signed an NDA.

00:18:36.516 --> 00:18:38.890
A third integration that
we wanted to bring up

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:41.680
is what we're calling
Docs Storage Integrations.

00:18:41.680 --> 00:18:45.140
Some of you may have heard
the announcement last year,

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:48.100
but with these new Docs
Storage Integrations,

00:18:48.100 --> 00:18:50.820
we're allowing users to use
the Google Docs editors--

00:18:50.820 --> 00:18:52.930
so Google Docs,
Sheets, and Slides--

00:18:52.930 --> 00:18:55.900
with the third party
storage solution of choice.

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:57.670
And so I'm curious
at this point, who

00:18:57.670 --> 00:19:00.400
here uses a third party
storage solution that's

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:03.175
not Google Drive?

00:19:03.175 --> 00:19:05.060
Cool, so great some
people in the audience.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:07.910
So this feature, hopefully,
will be very useful for you.

00:19:07.910 --> 00:19:11.250
So today, to help us see
what the value of this

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.080
could be for your
legal teams, we're

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.630
very excited to have
Raja Balakrishnana

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:18.270
from the first content--

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:20.847
first storage integration
partner we announced, Box.

00:19:20.847 --> 00:19:22.347
RAJA BALAKRISHANA:
Thank you, Allen.

00:19:25.756 --> 00:19:26.730
Hello, Everyone.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:27.700
I'm Raja Balakrishnan.

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.640
I'm a product manager
at Box, and I'm

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:32.820
working with our
counterparts at Google

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:35.400
to tightly integrate Box
and Google Docs, Sheets,

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:36.450
and Slides.

00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:39.000
So I'm very excited to
be here in front of you

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:42.120
to share with you on what we're
doing about the integration,

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:45.000
and also to give you
some insights on how

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:49.380
Box and Google together can
help you address your legal use

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:50.830
cases.

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:53.970
So before I jump
into that, I want

00:19:53.970 --> 00:19:56.100
to share with you what
Box does well, right,

00:19:56.100 --> 00:19:58.890
and how this integration
will benefit you.

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:03.360
So at a very high level, Box
lets you store your content,

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:06.090
secure your content,
share it, and govern it

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.720
all in one place, right.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:09.940
So that is what
primarily Box does.

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:13.260
But you spent the last 10
years building services

00:20:13.260 --> 00:20:17.290
that enable you, services
like compliance services,

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:19.950
reliability, security,
and so on and so forth,

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:23.280
so you could manage all
your content in one place,

00:20:23.280 --> 00:20:24.540
but at scale, right.

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:27.750
You can manage all
that content at scale.

00:20:27.750 --> 00:20:32.250
So what we're really working
on with the Google team here

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.920
is to, kind of,
make sure that you

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:38.670
get all the benefits of the
Google collaboration suites

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.500
coauthoring, editing,
and a lot of stuff

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:43.560
that we talked about
here in this conference,

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:47.160
but at the same time
maintain a centralized source

00:20:47.160 --> 00:20:49.320
of your truth so
all those changes

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:53.340
can be saved back directly
to Box in real time, right.

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:57.300
So what this means is you could
be within Box, if you're a Box

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:02.610
user, but you can still create
documents, slides, and sheets.

00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:03.750
You can open them.

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:06.210
You could collaborate and
share all that content

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:09.180
without ever having
to leave Box, right.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.890
Your content still
lives in Box, but you

00:21:10.890 --> 00:21:13.550
get all the collaboration
benefits of Google Docs,

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:14.460
Sheets, and Slides.

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:18.990
So that's what we're
working on this year.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:21.600
Before I jump into
some of the ideas

00:21:21.600 --> 00:21:25.560
that Box and Google can help you
specifically with on legal use

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:30.000
cases, we strongly believe
that the technologies that

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:32.970
are available to busy
legal professionals today

00:21:32.970 --> 00:21:34.086
are challenging, right.

00:21:34.086 --> 00:21:35.210
They're challenging to use.

00:21:35.210 --> 00:21:36.418
That are a few examples here.

00:21:36.418 --> 00:21:38.460
I'll just highlight
three of those.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:41.790
It's just not easy for
busy professionals,

00:21:41.790 --> 00:21:45.070
legal professionals to access
content on the go, right.

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:46.514
This could be mobile access.

00:21:46.514 --> 00:21:47.680
This could be remote access.

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:50.550
It's just not
easy-to-access content.

00:21:50.550 --> 00:21:54.060
Second, security sharing
and receiving content

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:54.870
is just not easy.

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:56.255
It's tedious, in fact.

00:21:56.255 --> 00:21:57.730
And I'll give you one example.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:22:01.500
Some of the lawyers in the
room might associate with this.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:04.470
I've observed that some
legal professionals still

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.121
put their case material
on CDs and ship it out

00:22:07.121 --> 00:22:08.120
to their clients, right.

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:10.080
So that kind of
stuff still happens.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:13.110
And technologies like FTP,
Extranet, all these legacy

00:22:13.110 --> 00:22:16.440
technologies, they are
definitely not easy to use,

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:19.560
they're not necessarily
secure, and in many cases,

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:21.040
they're not compliant, right.

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.970
So these are some of the
challenges that we think you

00:22:23.970 --> 00:22:27.010
or the teams that
you serve face.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:28.830
So let me give
you a few examples

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:33.330
of how Box and Google together
can help you address these.

00:22:33.330 --> 00:22:36.510
In the interest of time, I'll
just pick one use case, right.

00:22:36.510 --> 00:22:38.820
I'm going to pick
litigation case management,

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.940
but just like any other
collaboration use case,

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:44.610
you could imagine that this
would require collaboration

00:22:44.610 --> 00:22:46.020
with multiple parties.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:50.190
And these parties could be
collaborators within the team,

00:22:50.190 --> 00:22:52.860
these could be your attorneys,
these could be your paralegal

00:22:52.860 --> 00:22:54.180
folks, and so on and so forth.

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:56.670
This could also be your
clients, and in some cases

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:59.120
occasionally, this could
be your opposing counsel

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.950
with whom you'd have to
collaborate on content, right.

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.720
So this is the use case.

00:23:03.720 --> 00:23:06.420
And let me quickly tell you
how Box and Google can help

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.220
you address these use cases.

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:13.170
First of all, Box provides a
very highly organized folder

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:13.770
structure.

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:15.960
So what that means, why
is that relevant to you?

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:18.750
Your legal teams can
bring all their content

00:23:18.750 --> 00:23:21.240
into one centralized
location, right.

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.270
This could be all case material
relevant to a single client,

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:26.580
or it could be case
material across the cases

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:28.410
that your teams handled, right.

00:23:28.410 --> 00:23:30.120
In many cases, we
have noticed that this

00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:32.370
can help you
completely replace FTP

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:36.570
servers and other technologies
that are used for this purpose.

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:40.320
The second area that
we can jointly help you

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:44.130
is we offer a great permissions
model on documents and folders.

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.920
In fact, we offer seven
levels of permissions starting

00:23:46.920 --> 00:23:49.920
from preview only rights
all the way up to editing

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:50.940
rights on documents.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:54.510
So what this really means is
as a legal team, when you're

00:23:54.510 --> 00:23:56.700
working, when you're
thinking about collaboration,

00:23:56.700 --> 00:23:59.970
you could define who
has a view-only access

00:23:59.970 --> 00:24:02.670
to the document or the case
matter that you're working on,

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:03.610
but not edit, right.

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:06.750
So this is very important
in a collaboration use case.

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.180
And third-- and remember we
talked about the shipping CD

00:24:09.180 --> 00:24:10.880
use case-- we can
really help there,

00:24:10.880 --> 00:24:15.150
because Box and Google together
can help you share content--

00:24:15.150 --> 00:24:17.730
securely shared content--
not just with a few people,

00:24:17.730 --> 00:24:19.320
not just with
people in the room,

00:24:19.320 --> 00:24:23.760
but people across geographically
distributed in all locations.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.040
This could be clients
in many cases.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:29.130
And the reason
this is relevant is

00:24:29.130 --> 00:24:32.130
you guys deal with complicated
remote access technologies.

00:24:32.130 --> 00:24:33.650
They're just hard
to set up, right.

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:36.360
We heard about VPN
setting up VPNs

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:37.810
in the Keynote presentation.

00:24:37.810 --> 00:24:39.290
It's just really
hard to do that,

00:24:39.290 --> 00:24:40.831
and you don't have
to do that, right.

00:24:40.831 --> 00:24:43.950
Box and Google together can help
you address those use cases.

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:46.320
You could share, you
could collaborate content

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:47.950
very easily.

00:24:47.950 --> 00:24:51.660
But with all these capabilities,
these native content management

00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:54.180
capabilities of Box and the
collaboration capabilities

00:24:54.180 --> 00:24:56.770
that we're offering with
Google, what do you do

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:58.989
is you could change the
behavior of the teams

00:24:58.989 --> 00:25:00.030
that you're working with.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:01.500
I'll give you one example.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:05.530
Sending out attachments in
emails is not the way to do it,

00:25:05.530 --> 00:25:06.030
right.

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:07.980
Specifically in
your use case, you'd

00:25:07.980 --> 00:25:11.350
have to deal with multiple
versions of the same document,

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:11.850
right.

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:14.790
Staying on top of it with
sharing it over email

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:16.010
is just not easy.

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:18.090
With Box and Google
together, that's

00:25:18.090 --> 00:25:20.340
going to be extremely
easy for you, especially

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:23.730
your legal teams, to stay
on top of the case matter

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:25.560
even though there
are multiple versions

00:25:25.560 --> 00:25:27.310
that you'll have to deal with.

00:25:27.310 --> 00:25:31.190
And finally, in addition to
all the native management

00:25:31.190 --> 00:25:33.690
capabilities that I described
and the integration that we're

00:25:33.690 --> 00:25:38.340
doing with Google, we
also offer integrations

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:41.550
with documents signing
services like Docusign.

00:25:41.550 --> 00:25:44.210
Just like Google
integration, we at Box

00:25:44.210 --> 00:25:45.340
also have an integration.

00:25:45.340 --> 00:25:47.131
So that means that you
have a lot of choice

00:25:47.131 --> 00:25:50.820
to choose the right integration
for your specific use case.

00:25:50.820 --> 00:25:54.420
And for your specific
legal use cases, attorneys

00:25:54.420 --> 00:25:58.620
and paralegals and clients,
it's easier and faster for them

00:25:58.620 --> 00:26:00.570
to sign those documents.

00:26:00.570 --> 00:26:02.574
So in closing and
summary, I just

00:26:02.574 --> 00:26:03.990
