OK.

00:27:21.810 --> 00:27:23.080
MIKE CHASAN: Hi, everybody.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:26.130
My name is Mike Chasan,
and I am product counsel

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:26.890
for Google Docs.

00:27:26.890 --> 00:27:30.420
So I get to spend a lot
of time with these guys.

00:27:30.420 --> 00:27:35.190
Yeah, so when I started working
at Google six-plus years ago,

00:27:35.190 --> 00:27:37.590
I went through my first
day of Noogler orientation.

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:41.125
I learned all about the
Google tools and applications,

00:27:41.125 --> 00:27:42.000
and it was all great.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:43.380
I learned all about Google Docs.

00:27:43.380 --> 00:27:44.830
They had these
interactive demos.

00:27:44.830 --> 00:27:46.420
It was very exciting.

00:27:46.420 --> 00:27:49.140
And then on my second day, I
walked into the legal offices,

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:52.440
and they said, yeah, Google
Docs is great for internal use,

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:53.640
but you're a lawyer.

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:55.470
You need to put Word
on your computer.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:56.170
Do it now.

00:27:56.170 --> 00:27:58.230
It's the first thing.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:01.680
So that's what things were
like at the very beginning.

00:28:01.680 --> 00:28:06.060
About four years ago, I switched
to using Docs completely

00:28:06.060 --> 00:28:09.000
for my practice and
gave up Word entirely.

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:10.680
I might say that
I did it because I

00:28:10.680 --> 00:28:13.680
like these guys and the team
and I want to support them.

00:28:13.680 --> 00:28:16.740
The honest truth is the new
Pixel Chromebook came out,

00:28:16.740 --> 00:28:20.190
and I really wanted one, and
the only way I could get one

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:23.040
was if I committed to give up
my MacBook and my copy of Word.

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:26.110
So I got the new fancy computer.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:29.850
It does the Konami
code nights blinking,

00:28:29.850 --> 00:28:30.900
and I really like that.

00:28:30.900 --> 00:28:34.562
And so I just worked
around my issues with Docs

00:28:34.562 --> 00:28:35.520
and learned to love it.

00:28:35.520 --> 00:28:38.880
So I just wanted to give
you a few practice tips

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:40.620
that I've developed
over the years

00:28:40.620 --> 00:28:45.720
to help you make that transition
from using a mixed-mode Word

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:49.020
and Docs environment to
working completely in Docs.

00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:52.920
So the first point is
collaboration is king.

00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:55.800
The most compelling
use of Google Docs

00:28:55.800 --> 00:28:58.530
is to collaborate,
to get feedback,

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:01.320
to leave action items
for your teammates,

00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:03.960
to drop in questions and notes.

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:07.920
So even if you are working
in a Word document,

00:29:07.920 --> 00:29:10.447
and even if you are going to
keep it in a Word document,

00:29:10.447 --> 00:29:12.030
the first thing I
do when I get a Word

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:15.600
document is import it into
Docs and share it with my team.

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:18.330
That way I can consolidate
all that feedback,

00:29:18.330 --> 00:29:22.230
see what's going
on, and then proceed

00:29:22.230 --> 00:29:24.930
how I'm going to proceed
with the document.

00:29:24.930 --> 00:29:29.550
Workflows are also
really easy in Docs.

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:31.920
In advance of Next,
I had a lot of emails

00:29:31.920 --> 00:29:34.260
to review and approve,
a lot of blog posts

00:29:34.260 --> 00:29:35.580
to review and approve.

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:39.060
It's really easy in Docs to
drop a table, a list of everyone

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:42.090
that needs to approve a
document at the top of the file,

00:29:42.090 --> 00:29:44.370
and then everyone can
look at the same document

00:29:44.370 --> 00:29:45.254
at the same time.

00:29:45.254 --> 00:29:47.670
They can drop their names into
it when they've approved it

00:29:47.670 --> 00:29:48.289
and sign off.

00:29:48.289 --> 00:29:50.830
You don't have to worry about
which version you've looked at.

00:29:50.830 --> 00:29:52.770
And you can even look
at revision history

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:55.110
to see if the
document has changed

00:29:55.110 --> 00:29:57.330
since you were last in it.

00:29:57.330 --> 00:29:58.860
The other thing
to remember is not

00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:00.690
everything is collaborative.

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:02.280
Engineers love to collaborate.

00:30:02.280 --> 00:30:04.770
Lawyers don't like to
collaborate as well,

00:30:04.770 --> 00:30:06.810
or aren't as good at it.

00:30:06.810 --> 00:30:09.850
When working with
an external party,

00:30:09.850 --> 00:30:11.940
sometimes I find
it helpful to make

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:15.300
internal copies and external
copies of the same document.

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:20.070
That way my internal team can
discuss the document freely,

00:30:20.070 --> 00:30:23.520
put in comments, edits,
in the internal version.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:27.030
I can review that, consolidate,
come with the final position,

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:29.340
and bring that into
an external copy

00:30:29.340 --> 00:30:31.745
that I share with our partner.

00:30:31.745 --> 00:30:33.870
And then when I get comments
back from the partner,

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:35.411
I could do the same
thing in reverse,

00:30:35.411 --> 00:30:39.930
bring their comments back
for internal discussion.

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:43.410
The permission model that
Vishnu and Allen talked about

00:30:43.410 --> 00:30:47.940
is very helpful to
make sure that partners

00:30:47.940 --> 00:30:50.400
that only have view can't
see revision history.

00:30:50.400 --> 00:30:53.310
But sometimes when you
give them edit access,

00:30:53.310 --> 00:30:55.350
it's important to remember
that all editors can

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:56.790
see revision history.

00:30:56.790 --> 00:31:00.270
So if you make a new
copy of a document,

00:31:00.270 --> 00:31:02.310
it's a way of resetting
revision history

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:05.700
and making sure that
the new document doesn't

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:08.730
contain any of the past
comments and suggestions.

00:31:08.730 --> 00:31:12.990
And lastly, exporting to other
file formats is very easy.

00:31:12.990 --> 00:31:16.320
So if you do need to send an
attachment to somebody that

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:19.410
cannot work or will not work
in Google Docs, it's very easy.

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:22.080
It's one click away to
email out a Word version

00:31:22.080 --> 00:31:24.310
or a PDF version
of the document.

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:26.880
So I hope those were
some helpful tips that

00:31:26.880 --> 00:31:29.820
can help you guys
transition into using Docs

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.830
more in your legal practices.

