00:32:14.440 --> 00:32:15.607
of that in the first place.

00:32:15.607 --> 00:32:17.440
There's a detailed
session on this tomorrow,

00:32:17.440 --> 00:32:19.690
but I did want to cover
some very high level points.

00:32:19.690 --> 00:32:21.670
Because we actually
do have millions

00:32:21.670 --> 00:32:24.100
of customers that are
using our services,

00:32:24.100 --> 00:32:27.920
and they are doing it dealing
with regulated information.

00:32:27.920 --> 00:32:30.040
So the first thing that
I want to talk about

00:32:30.040 --> 00:32:32.180
is conducting a
compliance assessment.

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:37.250
Now, I've been at Google for
a little more than 10 years,

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:40.450
particularly with G Suite
from the very beginning,

00:32:40.450 --> 00:32:46.020
and we do not have the legal
experience or the compliance

00:32:46.020 --> 00:32:49.240
experience of all of our
customers around the world.

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:51.960
We have 190 so odd
countries, and let's say

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.420
we've gotten five
verticals in each country--

00:32:54.420 --> 00:32:57.360
financial services,
personal data, health care,

00:32:57.360 --> 00:33:01.080
manufacturing, investing.

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:03.870
That's, what, tens of
thousands, hundreds, millions

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:05.260
of different combinations.

00:33:05.260 --> 00:33:09.000
So our customers are the
experts in their own compliance

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:11.589
obligations and their own
compliance requirements.

00:33:11.589 --> 00:33:13.380
However, we have been
doing this very long,

00:33:13.380 --> 00:33:15.588
and we've worked with a lot
of regulatory authorities

00:33:15.588 --> 00:33:18.210
in helping to create
a product that

00:33:18.210 --> 00:33:22.230
can be adopted by as many
customers as possible worldwide

00:33:22.230 --> 00:33:25.200
in light of their regulatory
compliance obligations.

00:33:25.200 --> 00:33:27.550
So I would submit that when
you're reviewing Google--

00:33:27.550 --> 00:33:29.760
and I am bringing
these questions up,

00:33:29.760 --> 00:33:32.730
because we've addressed these
many, many times over the years

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:34.470
and have good answers--

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:36.237
you want to consider,
for example,

00:33:36.237 --> 00:33:38.070
what are the security
capabilities of Google

00:33:38.070 --> 00:33:41.045
with respect to what
you're doing on-site?

00:33:41.045 --> 00:33:43.170
You've probably seen some
of our security sessions.

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:45.390
We've got hundreds of
security engineers,

00:33:45.390 --> 00:33:46.830
but I'd submit
that in most cases

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:49.140
our security capabilities
will far exceed

00:33:49.140 --> 00:33:51.557
that of most of our customers.

00:33:51.557 --> 00:33:53.640
You have to think about
who owns the data that you

00:33:53.640 --> 00:33:55.110
put into the cloud.

00:33:55.110 --> 00:33:58.170
With G Suite and with Google,
your data remains yours.

00:33:58.170 --> 00:33:59.430
You place it into our systems.

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.110
That does not change anything.

00:34:02.110 --> 00:34:05.370
We also provide restrictions
on how data is used.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:07.140
We provide a data
processing amendment,

00:34:07.140 --> 00:34:10.409
which obligates us not
to use the data for ads.

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:11.550
No ads are seen.

00:34:11.550 --> 00:34:13.239
No data is used for ads.

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:15.030
We don't use the data
for any other purpose

00:34:15.030 --> 00:34:16.681
than to provide the services.

00:34:16.681 --> 00:34:19.139
That's particularly important
in the areas of, for example,

00:34:19.139 --> 00:34:21.397
European personal
data protection.

00:34:21.397 --> 00:34:22.980
Or when it comes to
the United States,

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:26.250
when it comes to FERPA, which
is the protection of student

00:34:26.250 --> 00:34:28.980
records or directory
information.

00:34:28.980 --> 00:34:34.050
So HIPAA, with regards to
placing PHI into our system,

00:34:34.050 --> 00:34:35.820
so data use restrictions.

00:34:35.820 --> 00:34:38.350
We provide obligation-- we
obligate ourselves to provide

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:42.360
you data incident notification,
which is very important in lots

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:45.360
of regulated markets and also
for the general data protection

00:34:45.360 --> 00:34:48.340
regulation, which is going to be
coming up in Europe next year,

00:34:48.340 --> 00:34:49.860
the GDPR.

00:34:49.860 --> 00:34:52.320
We provide an affirmation
that once you delete data,

00:34:52.320 --> 00:34:54.360
it will be purged
from our system.

00:34:54.360 --> 00:34:56.100
It will be gone, right.

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:57.450
It doesn't just stick around.

00:34:57.450 --> 00:35:00.630
I don't know how many people
on using on-prem software

00:35:00.630 --> 00:35:02.250
wipe their hard
drives every time

00:35:02.250 --> 00:35:04.470
they delete a file or document.

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:05.340
Data portability.

00:35:05.340 --> 00:35:08.820
We provide assurances that you
can take your data and go home,

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:12.009
if you want to leave G Suite.

00:35:12.009 --> 00:35:13.800
You also want to consider
what type of data

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:15.616
you're storing in
the system, right.

00:35:15.616 --> 00:35:17.490
As I mentioned, we have
millions of customers

00:35:17.490 --> 00:35:19.890
that store all types of data
that you want to make that

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:21.540
as part of your assessment.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:23.790
And then the last thing
I want to talk about

00:35:23.790 --> 00:35:25.800
with regards to the
compliance assessment

00:35:25.800 --> 00:35:28.860
is how do you know that I'm not
lying to you about everything

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:31.380
I say we do?

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:32.400
How do you know?

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:36.090
I could just be telling you
anything you want to hear.

00:35:36.090 --> 00:35:38.550
The way you know this
is because we undergo

00:35:38.550 --> 00:35:41.910
third-party audits of our
security and data protection

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:42.600
capabilities.

00:35:42.600 --> 00:35:45.540
We invite third-party auditors
to come in and examine

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:50.670
our processes against more than
100 controls to help ensure

00:35:50.670 --> 00:35:52.050
that what we do--

00:35:52.050 --> 00:35:54.550
what we say we do
actually what we do.

00:35:54.550 --> 00:35:57.630
So with respect to that,
we provide our customers

00:35:57.630 --> 00:35:58.980
with a SOC audit report.

00:35:58.980 --> 00:36:01.140
You see I mentioned
SOC 2 and SOC 3 where

00:36:01.140 --> 00:36:03.090
auditors, particularly
with a SOC 2 report,

00:36:03.090 --> 00:36:06.480
create a very detailed,
highly confidential assessment

00:36:06.480 --> 00:36:09.030
of our data protection
capabilities, which

00:36:09.030 --> 00:36:11.010
can be reviewed by your
chief security officer,

00:36:11.010 --> 00:36:13.560
chief risk officer, chief
information officer,

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:17.130
to be able to determine whether
our security capabilities align

00:36:17.130 --> 00:36:18.520
with your own requirements.

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:22.011
And I would submit that
I think that they might.

00:36:22.011 --> 00:36:24.510
But the nice thing about-- the
interesting thing about SOC 2

00:36:24.510 --> 00:36:26.220
is while it's a very
detailed assessment,

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:32.040
and it's 200 or 300 pages long,
to distinguish that from ISO

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.320
certification--
you'll see here nice--

00:36:34.320 --> 00:36:37.950
three nice little ISO
badges, ISO 27001,

00:36:37.950 --> 00:36:42.120
which is a security
standard, ISO 27017, which

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:46.950
is a brand new Cloud security
standard specific to Cloud,

00:36:46.950 --> 00:36:50.400
and then ISO 27018, which is
a new standard with relation

00:36:50.400 --> 00:36:53.680
to data protection,
specifically privacy.

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:57.450
So we provide SOC 2
type audits, but we also

00:36:57.450 --> 00:37:00.777
have achieved ISO
certification with respect

00:37:00.777 --> 00:37:01.860
to these three standards--

00:37:01.860 --> 00:37:04.560
27001, 17, and 18.

00:37:04.560 --> 00:37:08.370
It means we've attained a
certain level of compliance

00:37:08.370 --> 00:37:11.520
that was dictated not by
us, not by the industry,

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.140
but by the International
Organization

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:16.350
for Standardization,
a global body that

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:18.390
designs these certifications.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:19.980
The reason why we
use a global body

00:37:19.980 --> 00:37:23.610
is because it helps ensure
that as many customers as

00:37:23.610 --> 00:37:26.580
possible in as many
markets as possible

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:29.790
can leverage these third-party
audits and certifications.

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:31.800
And finally, we have
industry-specific and

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:34.590
region-specific regulatory
frameworks as well.

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:36.330
You heard me mention
HIPAA with regards

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:40.670
to the United States health
care information, PHI.

00:37:40.670 --> 00:37:42.840
We can accept HIPAA
information into G Suite

00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:44.820
into some of our services.

00:37:44.820 --> 00:37:47.430
We are also Fed ramp
certified, which

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:49.320
is very difficult
to obtain, which

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:52.410
allows US government agencies
to store data, Fed ramp

00:37:52.410 --> 00:37:54.550
moderate data into our system.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:57.330
And then finally, for our
many, many customers in Europe

00:37:57.330 --> 00:37:59.820
or those that have
or multinationals

00:37:59.820 --> 00:38:01.740
that have end
users in Europe, we

00:38:01.740 --> 00:38:05.130
offer data export mechanisms,
things like EU model contract

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:08.300
clauses, which we've offered
for customers for years.

00:38:08.300 --> 00:38:11.490
Incidentally, who here
heard about the invalidation

00:38:11.490 --> 00:38:13.710
of Safe Harbor?

00:38:13.710 --> 00:38:16.960
OK, that had to do
with whether Europeans

00:38:16.960 --> 00:38:19.270
were allowed to export
personal data from Europe

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:21.480
to the rest of the world.

00:38:21.480 --> 00:38:23.680
Safe Harbor was invalidated,
but model clauses

00:38:23.680 --> 00:38:25.480
was an alternative to
that which we offered

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:28.540
for years before this happened.

00:38:28.540 --> 00:38:29.380
Why?

00:38:29.380 --> 00:38:31.510
Because we know that model
clauses are important.

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:34.360
Data export is critical to
our customers in Europe,

00:38:34.360 --> 00:38:36.250
and so we've been
offering it since then.

00:38:36.250 --> 00:38:38.170
And we also implement
Privacy Shield--

00:38:38.170 --> 00:38:40.210
participate in Privacy
Shield as another way

00:38:40.210 --> 00:38:42.580
to allow data to be
exported from Europe.

00:38:42.580 --> 00:38:44.202
There are other
frameworks in the UK

00:38:44.202 --> 00:38:46.660
that we can talk about as well
or other parts of the world,

00:38:46.660 --> 00:38:47.830
but just the synopsis.

00:38:47.830 --> 00:38:49.330
And the last thing
I want to mention

00:38:49.330 --> 00:38:52.390
is I talked about all
the great things we do.

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:55.120
I talked about the third-party
audits and certifications

00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:58.360
that you can review to make sure
that we're not lying to you,

00:38:58.360 --> 00:39:01.450
but then we also need to
put it in writing, right.

00:39:01.450 --> 00:39:03.190
So our data processing
amendment is

00:39:03.190 --> 00:39:05.680
where you can find the
meat of a lot of the stuff.

00:39:05.680 --> 00:39:08.140
We obligate
ourselves to restrict

00:39:08.140 --> 00:39:10.567
how we process your data.

00:39:10.567 --> 00:39:12.400
This is where we promise
that we'll actually

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:14.740
do these third-party
audits and certifications.

00:39:14.740 --> 00:39:18.170
We have to promise it in
writing, otherwise, again,

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:19.960
it won't mean anything.

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:22.630
We promised to delete your
data when you want us to.

00:39:22.630 --> 00:39:24.520
We promised to provide
you notification--

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:27.190
I hope it doesn't happen,
but of data incidents.

00:39:27.190 --> 00:39:29.980
Because this is required for
many regulatory frameworks.

00:39:29.980 --> 00:39:31.910
If-- I hope it never
happens-- but if there

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:33.980
is a data incident with
your cloud provider,

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:35.350
you need to know about it.

00:39:35.350 --> 00:39:38.170
Because you may need to
fulfill your own regulatory

00:39:38.170 --> 00:39:39.160
obligations.

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:40.780
So we provide that commitment.

00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:42.430
We tell you about
any vendors we might

00:39:42.430 --> 00:39:44.740
use for sub-- for processing.

00:39:44.740 --> 00:39:46.600
We don't use very many
third-party vendors,

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:49.990
because we are Google, but for
technical support or customer

00:39:49.990 --> 00:39:51.100
support we may.

00:39:51.100 --> 00:39:53.680
And we also disclose
all of our subsidiaries

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:56.260
that are involved with
processing data, right.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:59.830
Because we have Google's
all over the world.

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:03.170
We outline our security
measures as well.

00:40:03.170 --> 00:40:06.340
So this is the combination
of compliance assessment

00:40:06.340 --> 00:40:08.380
where you can partner
with us, your compliance

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:10.630
assessment with our
expertise in the cloud,

00:40:10.630 --> 00:40:12.430
utilizing our
third-party auditing

00:40:12.430 --> 00:40:15.310
tools to verify
what we do, and then

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:18.280
finally the contractual
obligations that we provide

00:40:18.280 --> 00:40:21.707
to you to put in writing what
we say we're going to do.

00:40:21.707 --> 00:40:22.540
Thank you very much.

00:40:28.190 --> 00:40:29.990
ALLEN YANG: Thanks
very much, Mark.

00:40:29.990 --> 00:40:33.950
And our final guest is Ivan
Passos, product manager

00:40:33.950 --> 00:40:37.100
at Google Vault, which is a
very useful tool for dealing

00:40:37.100 --> 00:40:38.030
with litigation holds.

00:40:38.030 --> 00:40:40.560
So, Ivan.

00:40:40.560 --> 00:40:43.290
IVAN PASSOS: Thanks, Allen.

00:40:43.290 --> 00:40:46.650
So basically what
Google Vault does is it

00:40:46.650 --> 00:40:50.960
provides you the ability
to implement e-discovery

00:40:50.960 --> 00:40:53.700
and compliance into G Suite.

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:56.670
So if you're looking at
implementing legal documents

00:40:56.670 --> 00:41:00.720
and using documents for
your legal processes,

00:41:00.720 --> 00:41:03.330
they will be covered
by Google Vault,

00:41:03.330 --> 00:41:08.160
and they will have full
compliance checks and also

00:41:08.160 --> 00:41:11.040
the ability to generate those
documents in a legal case

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:13.320
or for any internal
investigations.

00:41:13.320 --> 00:41:17.670
What Google Vault does is
basically through retention

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:21.570
policies, you're able to define
exactly when your data should

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:24.960
be kept and for how long and
when your data should be gone,

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.310
and this is done automatically.

00:41:27.310 --> 00:41:29.790
Your IT managers will
be able to just set up

00:41:29.790 --> 00:41:31.250
those retention policies.

00:41:31.250 --> 00:41:34.860
They are done for each
specific data type.

00:41:34.860 --> 00:41:37.710
You can do that for G-mail,
for Docs, for Groups,

00:41:37.710 --> 00:41:39.710
for the different
applications in the G Suite,

00:41:39.710 --> 00:41:43.740
and you can set multiple
types of policies

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:45.580
of requirements and criteria.

00:41:45.580 --> 00:41:48.150
You can say that it should
be kept indefinitely

00:41:48.150 --> 00:41:51.330
for the legal team or for
three years for a job in et

00:41:51.330 --> 00:41:52.390
cetera, et cetera.

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:54.030
So we have a lot of
granularity there.

00:41:54.030 --> 00:41:56.850
And once you've set up
your proper policies

00:41:56.850 --> 00:41:59.850
for retention in data
lifecycle management,

00:41:59.850 --> 00:42:02.825
you can then search
for data as needed.

00:42:02.825 --> 00:42:04.200
You can search
for documents that

00:42:04.200 --> 00:42:07.110
are relevant for a specific
investigation or case.

00:42:07.110 --> 00:42:09.510
And once you code that
data and narrow it

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:13.230
down to the specific data
set you're looking for,

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:16.635
you can then export that
data either to provide

00:42:16.635 --> 00:42:18.750
to external counsel
or to move it

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:21.930
on to another investigation
aid discovery tool

00:42:21.930 --> 00:42:24.520
or to use it internally.

00:42:24.520 --> 00:42:26.850
Google Vault is part
of just G Suite.

00:42:26.850 --> 00:42:29.990
It's actually included in G
Suite business and G Suite

00:42:29.990 --> 00:42:31.050
enterprise.

00:42:31.050 --> 00:42:34.080
And this complements
what Allen and Vishnu

00:42:34.080 --> 00:42:36.480
were talking about
when you have document

00:42:36.480 --> 00:42:37.980
functionality for
your legal teams

00:42:37.980 --> 00:42:40.110
for producing contracts
and et cetera.

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:42.450
Google Vault provides
you full coverage

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:47.171
for e-discovery and compliance
for G Suite as a whole.

00:42:47.171 --> 00:42:47.670
That's it.

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:48.170
Thank you.

00:42:52.233 --> 00:42:54.570
ALLEN YANG: Thank you very much.

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:57.040
Thanks very much to all
of our guest speakers.

00:42:57.040 --> 00:43:01.445
So let's recap a little bit
about what we've seen today.

00:43:01.445 --> 00:43:02.820
At the beginning,
we first talked

00:43:02.820 --> 00:43:05.520
about a lot of the
features that Google Docs

00:43:05.520 --> 00:43:09.360
has to help you create very
well-formatted and powerful

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:11.400
legal documents.

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:13.410
We then talked a
little bit about what

00:43:13.410 --> 00:43:17.130
it's like to collaborate
on Google Docs,

00:43:17.130 --> 00:43:19.224
including touching
upon some features

00:43:19.224 --> 00:43:20.640
that you might
already be used to.

00:43:20.640 --> 00:43:23.340
Things like commenting
or suggestive changes,

00:43:23.340 --> 00:43:25.620
which is an improvement
upon red lining.

00:43:25.620 --> 00:43:28.230
Here we also talked about
various sharing controls

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:31.350
you have, and we talked about
file conversion fidelity, which

00:43:31.350 --> 00:43:33.990
is something we pay a
lot of attention to.

00:43:33.990 --> 00:43:37.200
And we also touched upon
the G Suite ecosystem,

00:43:37.200 --> 00:43:39.090
which offers a lot
of partner tools

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:42.870
which could help you
accomplish your legal needs.

00:43:42.870 --> 00:43:46.050
And then finally we talked
about the very important issues

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:50.070
of adoption, of security,
and of compliance,

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:53.610
and we had our three guest
speakers who brought some more

00:43:53.610 --> 00:43:56.130
color onto these topics in
the very black and white world

00:43:56.130 --> 00:43:59.010
of legal documents.

00:43:59.010 --> 00:44:00.960
So we wanted to
conclude by showing you

00:44:00.960 --> 00:44:03.089
this video about what
Iceland did with Google Docs.

00:44:03.089 --> 00:44:03.755
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:44:03.755 --> 00:44:06.210
- So, I'm Thorvaldur Gulfason,
Professor of Economics

00:44:06.210 --> 00:44:07.860
at the University of Iceland.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:11.760
I was one of the 25 members
of the constitutional assembly

00:44:11.760 --> 00:44:14.520
that adapted the post-crash
constitution for Iceland.

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:17.070
And we were delighted to
use Google Docs in order

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:20.040
to make our work easier.

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:24.810
On many, many occasions
we saved time and energy

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:28.592
by doing the drafting together,
not necessarily sitting

00:44:28.592 --> 00:44:29.300
in the same room.

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:32.820
We could be in different
places using Google Docs.

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:37.380
I'm convinced that our
constitution is a better

00:44:37.380 --> 00:44:41.820
document than it would've been
without these technological

00:44:41.820 --> 00:44:44.277
advantages.

00:44:44.277 --> 00:44:44.860
[END PLAYBACK]

00:44:44.860 --> 00:44:48.210
So yes, a country has actually
written their constitution

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:49.980
with Google Docs.

00:44:49.980 --> 00:44:52.966
So in conclusion,
we just want to say

00:44:52.966 --> 00:44:54.840
that we hope you'll go
back to your companies

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:58.320
and ask your legal teams and
all your super users of word

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:00.600
to give Google Docs a
shot, because we think

00:45:00.600 --> 00:45:02.970
they'll be very surprised
by just how much they're

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:03.847
capable of.

00:45:03.847 --> 00:45:04.680
Thank you very much.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:05.280
[APPLAUSE]

